@SP

I think that for some of us, setting standards for what is "cheating" is the way we like to solve.

Like many here, I started solving when the only way to do any serious cheating was to do the puzzle in a library.  By the time the internet arrived, I didn't need help.  But recent solvers have so much temptation!

@Hitch
Try doing an early week puzzle using only the across clues or only the down clues. I didn't succeed today, but close. It can be fun. Hint: if you're using the app, turn off the clue bars. 

TIL mare's nest.

@Mike

Anyone who doesn’t get your post obviously can’t take an oak. (Even though it might have been acorny one).

Hey everyone, Mike is apparently too modest to toot his own horn, but if you like his wit and humor check out his own puzzles on Crosshare under the moniker Mikey G. Many of them are meta puzzles where there’s a trick to solve after the puzzle, they are quite genius and also display a lot of his cleverness and punniness. You will like them!

@Bill in Yokohama

Here’s the latest one. Once you find one, click on his nane and click follow, you will get an email every time he posts a new one; and if you click on his name you can also see all his older puzzles, which you can find under your information when you see who you are following. 

<a href="https://crosshare.org/crosswords/gRW9bMaSssSgN9FTUa4W/glean-sweep" target="_blank">https://crosshare.org/crosswords/gRW9bMaSssSgN9FTUa4W/glean-sweep</a>

@Charlie 
I've heard it before, but not in a long time, and I'm pretty sure I've never used it.
According to Perplexity, the original expression, “to find a mare’s nest” meant to think you had discovered something remarkable, only for it to prove imaginary, worthless, or absurd. Over time it shifted to mean a mess, though it can still mean a hoax or fraud.

