@Lewis
I've often wondered if it's a combination of mistranslation + back-formation from the French 'cauchemar' = nightmare, equating 'cauche' with Eng. 'couch' and from there to any cozy shelter, i.e., 'nest'.
I'm only familiar with the older meaning and so the clue was zero use for me. And I tend to think of it as a Britishism since I've only seen it in UK literature.

